KG42.register("rules-53bcf6d4c920b6d154d33c6c",{"id":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R001","paper":"07-nash_bargaining","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R001","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","name":"Inter-area market clearing","process_stage":"clearing","market_meaning":"Social operating cost minimization in P1 yields optimal energy scheduling and inter-microgrid energy allocations for all microgrids. Scope: Day-ahead energy allocation among interconnected microgrids; P1 covers all microgrids in M. Condition: P1 is solved subject to (1)–(3), (5), (6), (8)–(11), (14), (15), and (17). The virtual clearing house acts as the information-receiving and signal-updating coordinator that provides inter-microgrid energy clearing. Scope: Energy-allocation component of the virtual clearing-house service for interconnected microgrids at the beginning of daily operation. Condition: During the distributed solution of P1. Bilateral energy allocations must balance: the two microgrids' signed quantities for their mutual trade must sum to zero in each time slot. Scope: Inter-microgrid energy allocations for every distinct microgrid pair and every time slot. Inter-microgrid allocations must satisfy each microgrid's time-slot power balance, with net traded energy included on the supply side alongside local generation, main-grid purchases and storage discharge. Scope: Inter-microgrid allocations within each microgrid's power balance, for every microgrid and time slot. Condition: Microgrids are located close to each other, so energy-exchange losses are assumed negligible. The P1 iteration terminates when the aggregate discrepancy between auxiliary and microgrid energy-trading schedules meets the stated tolerance. Scope: The P1 energy-allocation iteration in Step 1 of Algorithm 1. Condition: ∑_{i∈M} ‖ ê_i(k) − e_i(k) ‖ ≤ ϵ1.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function clearing.inter_area under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["clearing.inter_area","clearing.social_welfare","coordination.local_operator"],"E":[{"edge_id":"v141-c-R001-E001","source":"clearing.social_welfare","target":"clearing.inter_area","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R001-C001"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E17","v141-b-g02-E18"]},{"edge_id":"v141-c-R001-E002","source":"coordination.local_operator","target":"clearing.inter_area","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R001-C002"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E37"]}],"omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Day-ahead energy allocation among interconnected microgrids; P1 covers all microgrids in M. | Energy-allocation component of the virtual clearing-house service for interconnected microgrids at the beginning of daily operation. | Inter-microgrid allocations within each microgrid's power balance, for every microgrid and time slot. | Inter-microgrid energy allocations for every distinct microgrid pair and every time slot. | The P1 energy-allocation iteration in Step 1 of Algorithm 1.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e19","v141-b-g01-e20","v141-b-g02-E17","v141-b-g02-E18","v141-b-g02-E35","v141-b-g02-E36","v141-b-g02-E37"]},"x":[{"variable_id":"x_e_ij","symbol":"e_{i,j}^{t}","meaning":"Energy that microgrid i exchanges with microgrid j in time slot t; positive if i purchases from j, negative if i sells to j.","kind":"operational","domain":"\\mathbb{R} (signed energy exchange)","unit":"kW","indices":["i","j","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e19","v141-b-g01-e27","v141-b-g02-E09"]},{"variable_id":"x_e_i","symbol":"e_i","meaning":"Energy trading vector of microgrid i over all time slots and other microgrids, e_i = {e_{i,j}^t, for all t in T, j in M\\i}.","kind":"operational","domain":"collection of signed bilateral energy exchanges e_{i,j}^t","unit":"kW","indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e27","v141-b-g02-E36"]},{"variable_id":"x_e_hat_i","symbol":"\\hat{e}_i","meaning":"Auxiliary variable vector replicating microgrid i's energy-trading decisions in the distributed P1 solution.","kind":"operational","domain":"collection of auxiliary bilateral energy exchanges \\hat{e}_{i,j}^t for t in T, j in M\\i","unit":"kW","indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E23","v141-b-g02-E35","v141-b-g03-ev-07"]},{"variable_id":"x_g_i","symbol":"g_i^{t}","meaning":"Local wind power supply used by microgrid i in time slot t.","kind":"operational","domain":"0 \u003c= g_i^t \u003c= eta_i^t G_i","unit":"kW","indices":["i","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e07"]},{"variable_id":"x_q_i","symbol":"q_i","meaning":"Main-grid power schedule of microgrid i, q_i = {q_{b,i}, q_{s,i}}.","kind":"operational","domain":"collection of q_{b,i}^t and q_{s,i}^t over the horizon","unit":"kW","indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e21"]},{"variable_id":"x_q_b_i","symbol":"q_{b,i}^{t}","meaning":"Power bought by microgrid i from the main grid in time slot t.","kind":"operational","domain":"0 \u003c= q_{b,i}^t \u003c= Q_{b,i}^{max}","unit":"kW","indices":["i","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e08"]},{"variable_id":"x_q_s_i","symbol":"q_{s,i}^{t}","meaning":"Power sold by microgrid i to the main grid in time slot t.","kind":"operational","domain":"0 \u003c= q_{s,i}^t \u003c= Q_{s,i}^{max}","unit":"kW","indices":["i","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09"]},{"variable_id":"x_r_c_i","symbol":"r_{c,i}^{t}","meaning":"Charging power of the energy storage in microgrid i in time slot t.","kind":"operational","domain":"0 \u003c= r_{c,i}^t \u003c= r_{c,i}^{max}","unit":"kW","indices":["i","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e12"]},{"variable_id":"x_r_d_i","symbol":"r_{d,i}^{t}","meaning":"Discharging power of the energy storage in microgrid i in time slot t.","kind":"operational","domain":"0 \u003c= r_{d,i}^t \u003c= r_{d,i}^{max}","unit":"kW","indices":["i","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e12"]},{"variable_id":"x_x_n","symbol":"x_n^{t}","meaning":"Elastic power consumption of user n in microgrid i in time slot t.","kind":"operational","domain":"d_n^{t,min} \u003c= x_n^t \u003c= d_n^{t,max}, with sum_t x_n^t = D_n","unit":"kW","indices":["n","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e10"]}],"theta":[{"parameter_id":"theta_M","symbol":"\\mathcal{M}","meaning":"Set of all interconnected microgrids.","value":{"kind":"text","number_value":null,"text_value":"M = {1, ..., M}"},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e02"]},{"parameter_id":"theta_T","symbol":"\\mathcal{T}","meaning":"Set of time slots in the daily operation horizon.","value":{"kind":"text","number_value":null,"text_value":"T = {1, ..., T}, with T = 24"},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e03"]},{"parameter_id":"theta_N_i","symbol":"\\mathcal{N}_i","meaning":"Set of users in microgrid i.","value":{"kind":"text","number_value":null,"text_value":"set of users in microgrid i"},"unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e10"]},{"parameter_id":"theta_eps1","symbol":"\\epsilon_1","meaning":"Error tolerance for the terminal condition of the P1 distributed clearing iteration.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"\\epsilon_1 > 0"},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E35","v141-b-g02-E36"]},{"parameter_id":"theta_b_i","symbol":"b_i^{t}","meaning":"Aggregate inelastic load of all users in microgrid i in time slot t.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"exogenous aggregate inelastic load"},"unit":"kW","indices":["i","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e17"]}],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R001-C001","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Social operating cost minimization in P1 yields optimal energy scheduling and inter-microgrid energy allocations for all microgrids. Scope: Day-ahead energy allocation among interconnected microgrids; P1 covers all microgrids in M. Condition: P1 is solved subject to (1)–(3), (5), (6), (8)–(11), (14), (15), and (17).","expression":"\\min_{\\{g_i,q_i,x_n,r_{c,i},r_{d,i},e_i\\}_{i\\in\\mathcal{M}}} \\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal{M}} CO_i(q_i,x_n,r_{c,i},r_{d,i}) \\quad \\text{subject to } (1)\\text{--}(3),(5),(6),(8)\\text{--}(11),(14),(15),(17)","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.social_welfare","clearing.inter_area"],"variable_refs":["x_g_i","x_q_i","x_x_n","x_r_c_i","x_r_d_i","x_e_i"],"parameter_refs":["theta_M"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E17","v141-b-g02-E18"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R001-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The virtual clearing house acts as the information-receiving and signal-updating coordinator that provides inter-microgrid energy clearing. Scope: Energy-allocation component of the virtual clearing-house service for interconnected microgrids at the beginning of daily operation. Condition: During the distributed solution of P1.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator","clearing.inter_area"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E37"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R001-C003","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Bilateral energy allocations must balance: the two microgrids' signed quantities for their mutual trade must sum to zero in each time slot. Scope: Inter-microgrid energy allocations for every distinct microgrid pair and every time slot.","expression":"e_{i,j}^{t} + e_{j,i}^{t} = 0, \\quad \\forall t \\in \\mathcal{T},\\ \\forall j \\in \\mathcal{M}\\setminus\\{i\\},\\ \\forall i \\in \\mathcal{M}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.inter_area"],"variable_refs":["x_e_ij"],"parameter_refs":["theta_M","theta_T"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e19"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R001-C004","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Inter-microgrid allocations must satisfy each microgrid's time-slot power balance, with net traded energy included on the supply side alongside local generation, main-grid purchases and storage discharge. Scope: Inter-microgrid allocations within each microgrid's power balance, for every microgrid and time slot. Condition: Microgrids are located close to each other, so energy-exchange losses are assumed negligible.","expression":"g_i^{t} + q_{b,i}^{t} + r_{d,i}^{t} + \\sum_{j\\in\\mathcal{M}\\setminus\\{i\\}} e_{i,j}^{t} = q_{s,i}^{t} + r_{c,i}^{t} + b_i^{t} + \\sum_{n\\in\\mathcal{N}_i} x_n^{t}, \\quad \\forall t\\in\\mathcal{T},\\ \\forall i\\in\\mathcal{M}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.inter_area"],"variable_refs":["x_g_i","x_q_b_i","x_r_d_i","x_e_ij","x_q_s_i","x_r_c_i","x_x_n"],"parameter_refs":["theta_M","theta_T","theta_N_i","theta_b_i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e20"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R001-C005","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The P1 iteration terminates when the aggregate discrepancy between auxiliary and microgrid energy-trading schedules meets the stated tolerance. Scope: The P1 energy-allocation iteration in Step 1 of Algorithm 1. Condition: ∑_{i∈M} ‖ ê_i(k) − e_i(k) ‖ ≤ ϵ1.","expression":"\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal{M}} \\lVert \\hat{e}_i(k) - e_i(k) \\rVert \\le \\epsilon_1","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.inter_area"],"variable_refs":["x_e_i","x_e_hat_i"],"parameter_refs":["theta_M","theta_eps1"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E35","v141-b-g02-E36"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e19","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p05-c2r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.C Energy Trading Among Microgrids","anchor_text":"NECTED MICROGRIDS 2651 ue The energy trading and payment among microgrids should di- satisfy the mar","equation_id":"(15)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The energy trading and payment among microgrids should satisfy the market clearing constraints: e_{i,j}^t + e_{j,i}^t = 0, ∀t ∈ T, ∀j ∈ M\\i, ∀i ∈ M, (15)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e20","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.C Energy Trading Among Microgrids","anchor_text":"o- j∈M\\i n∈Ni ed ∀t ∈T , ∀i ∈M, (17) nd where j∈M\\i eti,j is the net energy traded between microgrid","equation_id":"(17)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"We assume that the microgrids are located close to each other, such that the loss of energy exchange is negligible. The new power balance constraint for each microgrid is written as g_i^t + q_{b,i}^t + r_{d,i}^t + Σ_{j∈M\\i} e_{i,j}^t = q_{s,i}^t + r_{c,i}^t + b_i^t + Σ_{n∈N_i} x_n^t, ∀t ∈ T, ∀i ∈ M, (17) where Σ_{j∈M\\i} e_{i,j}^t is the net energy traded between microgrid i and all other microgrids in time slot t. If Σ_{j∈M\\i} e_{i,j}^t > 0, microgrid i purchases energy from other microgrids to serve its local demand; otherwise, Σ_{j∈M\\i} e_{i,j}^t \u003c 0, microgrid i sells energy to make profit."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E17","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p06-c2r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":"2652","section":"IV. PROBLEM ANALYSIS","anchor_text":"BP can be decomposed into two sequential subproblems on energy d- trading & scheduling and trading p","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Theorem 1: The energy-trading bargaining problem NBP can be decomposed into two sequential subproblems on energy trading & scheduling and trading payment, denoted as P1 and P2, respectively. In the first step, we solve a social operating cost minimization problem P1 for the interconnected-microgrids system, which yields the optimal energy scheduling and energy trading for all microgrids. Those microgrids that trade energy with other microgrids form the set M′. In the second step, microgrids in set M′ bargain with each other to determine the energy-trading payments in P2."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E18","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p06-c2r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":"2652","section":"IV. PROBLEM ANALYSIS","anchor_text":"BP can be decomposed into two sequential subproblems on energy d- trading & scheduling and trading p","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"P1: Social operating cost minimization problem:\nmin ∑_{i∈M} CO_i(q_i, x_n, r_{c,i}, r_{d,i})\nsubject to (1)–(3), (5), (6), (8)–(11), (14), (15), and (17),\nvariables: { g_i, q_i, x_n, r_{c,i}, r_{d,i}, e_i, i ∈ M }.\nSolving P1 determines the optimal energy trading & scheduling {g_i^*, q_i^*, x_n^*, r_{c,i}^*, r_{d,i}^*, e_i^*} for each microgrid i ∈ M. If a microgrid i has a nonzero energy trading vector e_i, then it belongs to set M′, and will participate in the payment bargaining in Problem P2. For the non-trading microgrids j ∈ M\\M′, they do not participate in the payment bargaining and their operating costs are the same as the disagreement points C_j^{Non}."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E35","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p08-c2r1__v141-a-M01-activation","page":8,"printed_page":"2654","section":"V.C Algorithm Design and Implementation","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON SMART GRID, VOL. 9, NO. 4, JULY 2018 Algorithm 1 Distributed Algorithm Solving ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Algorithm 1 Distributed Algorithm Solving P1 and P2\n1: Step 1: solve Problem P1.\n2: Initialization: iteration index k = 1, error tolerance ϵ1 > 0, stepsize ρ1(0) = 1, and initial multipliers λ(0) = 0.\n3: repeat\n4: At k-th iteration,\n5: Lower Level Problem: Microgrid i solves Problem P1-MGi based on the current value of dual variables λ(k);\n6: Higher Level Problem: The virtual clearing house makes update according to (21) and (22).\n7: Update iteration index k = k + 1;\n8: until terminal condition is satisfied ...\n9: Step 2: solve Problem P2.\n10: Initialization: iteration index k = 1, error tolerance ϵ2 > 0, stepsize ρ2(0) = 1, and initial multipliers γ(0) = 0.\n11: repeat\n12: At k-th iteration,\n13: Lower Level Problem: Microgrid i solves Problem P2-MGi based on the current value of dual variables γ(k);\n14: Higher Level Problem: The virtual clearing house makes updates according to the rules in (25) and (26).\n15: Update iteration index k = k + 1;\n16: until terminal condition is satisfied ...\n17: end"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E36","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p08-c2r2__v141-a-M01-activation","page":8,"printed_page":"2654","section":"V.C Algorithm Design and Implementation","anchor_text":"7: Update iteration index k = k + 1; 8: until terminal condition is satisﬁed, i.e., i∈M ∥ˆei(k) − ei","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"8: until terminal condition is satisfied, i.e., ∑_{i∈M} ‖ ê_i(k) − e_i(k) ‖ ≤ ϵ1.\n...\n16: until terminal condition is satisfied, i.e., ∑_{i∈M′} ‖ π̂_i(k) − π_i(k) ‖ ≤ ϵ2."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E37","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p08-c2r4__v141-a-M01-activation","page":8,"printed_page":"2654","section":"V.C Algorithm Design and Implementation","anchor_text":"To implement the solution method for solving the energy trading and payment problems P1 and P2, we d","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"We introduce the concept of virtual clearing house for solving the higher level problem. The virtual clearing house is a communication and computing module, which provides clearing service of energy trading and payment for all the microgrids. At the beginning of each daily operation, the virtual clearing house updates the auxiliary and dual variables according to (21), (22) and (25), (26), and broadcasts to all participating microgrids when solving P1 and P2, respectively. All the participating microgrids concurrently solve P1-MGi and P2-MGi and report their energy trading schedules and payments to the virtual clearing house."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e02","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p02-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. INTERCONNECTED-MICROGRIDS SYSTEM","anchor_text":"g ts. interconnected-microgrids system and jointly optimize both We their internal power scheduling ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"We consider a network of M interconnected microgrids M = {1, ..., M}. These microgrids are connected to the main power grid, and are also interconnected with each other. These interconnected microgrids can exchange power and information with each other through a power bus and a communication network. Fig. 1 illustrates such a system model."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e03","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p02-c2r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. INTERCONNECTED-MICROGRIDS SYSTEM","anchor_text":"ed a communication network. Fig. 1 illustrates such a system cal model. Each microgrid i ∈M contains","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Each microgrid i ∈ M contains the following components: local renewable generation, energy storage, and demand responsive users. We consider an operation horizon of one day, which is divided into T = 24 equal time slots, denoted as T = {1, ..., T}. We assume that microgrids can schedule their power supplies and flexible loads based on the prediction of daily renewable energy profiles, and any mismatch caused by the prediction errors can be balanced in the real-time market. ... 1Microgrid can work either in the island mode or in the connected mode. In the island mode, the microgrid is isolated from the main grid, and only relies on its local generation as the supply. In the connected mode, the microgrid is connected to the main grid, and can purchase power from the main grid. We assume that the microgrid works in the connected mode in this paper."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e07","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p03-c1r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.A Power Scheduling in Each Microgrid","anchor_text":"p g i i g p co kW capacity. We assume that microgrid i has installed wind ov turbine generators with","equation_id":"(1)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"We assume that microgrid i has installed wind turbine generators with total generation capacity G_i (kW), and have the following constraint for the wind power supply g_i = {g_i^t, ∀t ∈ T}: 0 ≤ g_i^t ≤ η_i^t G_i, ∀t ∈ T, ∀i ∈ M, (1) where η_i^t G_i denotes the maximum available wind power of microgrid i in time slot t. Note that the local wind power usage g_i^t of microgrid i in time slot t can be less but no greater than the available wind power η_i^t G_i, because the microgrid operator can either curtail excess wind power generation or sell excess generation back to the main grid."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e08","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p03-c2r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.A Power Scheduling in Each Microgrid","anchor_text":"NECTED MICROGRIDS 2649 Different from conventional power generations, wind power generation does not","equation_id":"(2)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"When the local wind power generation is not adequate to meet the demand, microgrid i can also purchase power from the main power grid. Let q_{b,i}^t denote the power bought from the main grid by microgrid i in time slot t, and define q_{b,i} = {q_{b,i}^t, ∀t ∈ T}. The purchased power q_{b,i} is subject to the following constraint: 0 ≤ q_{b,i}^t ≤ Q_{b,i}^{max}, ∀t ∈ T, ∀i ∈ M, (2) where Q_{b,i}^{max} denotes the maximum amount of power that microgrid i can purchase from the main grid, due to physical capacity limit."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e09","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p03-c2r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.A Power Scheduling in Each Microgrid","anchor_text":"0 ≤qb,i ≤Qb,i , ∀t ∈T , ∀i ∈M, (2) where Qmaxb,i denotes the maximum amount of power that microgrid ","equation_id":"(3)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"When the microgrid has excess local wind power generation, it can sell power back to the main grid under a feed-in tariff contract [18]. Let q_{s,i}^t denote the power sold to the main grid by microgrid i in time slot t, and define q_{s,i} = {q_{s,i}^t, ∀t ∈ T}. The power sold to the main grid q_{s,i} satisfies the following constraint: 0 ≤ q_{s,i}^t ≤ Q_{s,i}^{max}, ∀t ∈ T, ∀i ∈ M, (3) where Q_{s,i}^{max} denotes the maximum allowed amount of power that microgrid i can sell to the main grid."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e10","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p03-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.A Power Scheduling in Each Microgrid","anchor_text":"conditioning) and washer demands, can be ﬂexibly scheduled nd over time. For user n ∈Ni in microgrid","equation_id":"(5)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The demand response program can only control the elastic loads, and should be subject to the following constraints: Σ_{t∈T} x_n^t = D_n, ∀n ∈ N_i, ∀i ∈ M, (5) d_n^{t,min} ≤ x_n^t ≤ d_n^{t,max}, ∀t ∈ T, ∀n ∈ N_i, ∀i ∈ M, (6) where constraint (5) corresponds to the prescribed total energy requirement D_n in the entire operation horizon. Constraint (6) provides a lower bound d_n^{t,min} and upper bound d_n^{t,max} for the power consumption of user n in each time slot t."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e12","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p04-c1r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.A Power Scheduling in Each Microgrid","anchor_text":"user n as de Cn(xn) = βn xtn −ytn 2, (7) ho t∈T B. where (xtn −ytn)2 measures how much the actual po","equation_id":"(8)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"First, the charging and discharging power in each time slot t are bounded, and satisfy the following constraints: 0 ≤ r_{c,i}^t ≤ r_{c,i}^{max}, ∀t ∈ T, ∀i ∈ M, (8) 0 ≤ r_{d,i}^t ≤ r_{d,i}^{max}, ∀t ∈ T, ∀i ∈ M, (9) where r_{c,i}^{max} and r_{d,i}^{max} denote the maximum charging and discharging rates of energy storage in microgrid i, respectively."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e17","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p04-c2r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.B Single Microgrid's Cost Minimization Problem","anchor_text":"c,i { c,i, T } d,i { d,i, T } denote the charging and discharging amount over the operation 7) horiz","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"First of all, the microgrid operator should keep the power supply and demand balanced, i.e., g_i^t + q_{b,i}^t + r_{d,i}^t = q_{s,i}^t + r_{c,i}^t + b_i^t + Σ_{n∈N_i} x_n^t, ∀t ∈ T, ∀i ∈ M, (13) where the left-hand side represents the total power supply in time slot t, including wind power generation g_i^t, power drawn from the main grid q_{b,i}^t, and power discharged from the battery r_{d,i}^t. The right-hand side represents the total power demand in time slot t, including power sold to the main grid q_{s,i}^t, power charged into the battery r_{c,i}^t, aggregate inelastic load b_i^t and aggregate elastic load of all the users Σ_{n∈N_i} x_n^t."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e21","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.A Power Scheduling in Each Microgrid","anchor_text":"ch (buying) price and denote pts as the power feed-in (selling) price3 set by the main grid in time ","equation_id":"(4)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"We denote p_b^t as the power procurement (buying) price and denote p_s^t as the power feed-in (selling) price set by the main grid in time slot t. For notational simplicity, we let q_i = {q_{b,i}, q_{s,i}} denote the main grid power schedule of microgrid i over T time slots. The energy cost of microgrid i is written as C_i(q_i) = Σ_{t∈T} ( p_b^t q_{b,i}^t − p_s^t q_{s,i}^t ), ∀i ∈ M. (4) 3We assume that all the microgrids are connected to the same main grid, and the power buying/selling prices for all the microgrids are the same."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e27","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.C Energy Trading Among Microgrids","anchor_text":"We consider that each microgrid i ∈M bargains with all the other interconnected microgrids to determ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"We consider that each microgrid i ∈ M bargains with all the other interconnected microgrids to determine the amount of energy trading e_i = {e_{i,j}^t, ∀t ∈ T, ∀j ∈ M\\i} and the associated payment π_i = {π_{i,j}, ∀j ∈ M\\i}. Here e_{i,j}^t denotes the amount of energy that microgrid i exchanges with microgrid j in time slot t, and π_{i,j} denotes the associated payment for energy trading between microgrid i and microgrid j. If microgrid i purchases energy from microgrid j in time slot t, then e_{i,j}^t > 0; otherwise, microgrid i sells energy to microgrid j and e_{i,j}^t \u003c 0. Similarly, if microgrid i makes payment to microgrid j, then π_{i,j} > 0; otherwise microgrid i receives payment from microgrid j and π_{i,j} \u003c 0. 4Note that the payments π_i are determined through bargaining between microgrids, and are not necessary linear in terms of the energy exchange e_i."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E09","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p05-c2r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":"2651","section":"III.C Energy Trading Among Microgrids","anchor_text":"NECTED MICROGRIDS 2651 ue The energy trading and payment among microgrids should di- satisfy the mar","equation_id":"(15)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"e_{i,j}^t + e_{j,i}^t = 0,   ∀t ∈ T , ∀j ∈ M\\i, ∀i ∈ M,   (15)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E23","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p07-c1r4__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":7,"printed_page":"2653","section":"V.A Solving P1 (Social Cost Minimization)","anchor_text":"i { i,j, j \\ , T }, p constraints (15) by ˆeti,j = eti,j, ∀t ∈T , ∀j ∈M\\i, ∀i ∈M, (19) an ˆeti,j + ˆ","equation_id":"(19)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Specifically, let us introduce auxiliary variables ê_i = {ê_{i,j}^t, ∀j ∈ M\\i, ∀t ∈ T }, and replace constraints (15) by\nê_{i,j}^t = e_{i,j}^t,   ∀t ∈ T , ∀j ∈ M\\i, ∀i ∈ M,   (19)\nê_{i,j}^t + ê_{j,i}^t = 0,   ∀t ∈ T , ∀j ∈ M\\i, ∀i ∈ M.   (20)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-ev-07","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p08-c2r1__v141-a-M01-activation","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"V.C Algorithm Design and Implementation","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON SMART GRID, VOL. 9, NO. 4, JULY 2018 Algorithm 1 Distributed Algorithm Solving ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Algorithm 1 Distributed Algorithm Solving P1 and P2\n 1: Step 1: solve Problem P1.\n 2: Initialization: iteration index k = 1, error tolerance ϵ1 > 0, stepsize ρ1(0) = 1, and initial multipliers λ(0) = 0.\n 3: repeat\n 4: At k-th iteration,\n 5: Lower Level Problem: Microgrid i solves Problem P1-MGi based on the current value of dual variables λ(k);\n 6: Higher Level Problem: The virtual clearing house makes update according to (21) and (22).\n 7: Update iteration index k = k + 1;\n 8: until terminal condition is satisfied, i.e., ∑_{i∈M} ∥ˆei(k) − ei(k)∥ ≤ ϵ1."}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":null,"empty_x_reason":null,"empty_theta_reason":null},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M01-activation","v141-a-M01-clearing","v141-a-M01-coordination","v141-a-M01-pricing","v141-a-M01-validation"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"clearing.inter_area","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E17","quote":"In the first step, we solve a social operating cost minimization problem P1 for the interconnected-microgrids system, which yields the optimal energy scheduling and energy trading for all microgrids."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E18","quote":"P1: Social operating cost minimization problem: min ∑_{i∈M} CO_i(q_i, x_n, r_{c,i}, r_{d,i}) subject to (1)–(3), (5), (6), (8)–(11), (14), (15), and (17),"}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E37","quote":"The virtual clearing house is a communication and computing module, which provides clearing service of energy trading and payment for all the microgrids."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E37","quote":"At the beginning of each daily operation, the virtual clearing house updates the auxiliary and dual variables according to (21), (22) and (25), (26), and broadcasts to all participating microgrids when solving P1 and P2, respectively."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E37","quote":"All the participating microgrids concurrently solve P1-MGi and P2-MGi and report their energy trading schedules and payments to the virtual clearing house."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e19","quote":"The energy trading and payment among microgrids should satisfy the market clearing constraints: e_{i,j}^t + e_{j,i}^t = 0, ∀t ∈ T, ∀j ∈ M\\i, ∀i ∈ M, (15)"}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e20","quote":"We assume that the microgrids are located close to each other, such that the loss of energy exchange is negligible. The new power balance constraint for each microgrid is written as g_i^t + q_{b,i}^t + r_{d,i}^t + Σ_{j∈M\\i} e_{i,j}^t = q_{s,i}^t + r_{c,i}^t + b_i^t + Σ_{n∈N_i} x_n^t, ∀t ∈ T, ∀i ∈ M, (17) where Σ_{j∈M\\i} e_{i,j}^t is the net energy traded between microgrid i and all other microgrids in time slot t."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E35","quote":"Algorithm 1 Distributed Algorithm Solving P1 and P2 1: Step 1: solve Problem P1."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E36","quote":"8: until terminal condition is satisfied, i.e., ∑_{i∈M} ‖ ê_i(k) − e_i(k) ‖ ≤ ϵ1."}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"e065963fbdf32befa23f95ae4ae2e7fd37d687ea16072328e3483102a78ff29c","applicability":"formalized","reason":"The rule includes source-supported mathematical expressions for the P1 social-cost objective (C001), the bilateral energy market-clearing equation (15) (C003), the inter-microgrid power balance (17) (C004), and the P1 terminal residual condition (C005). C002 is a procedural coordination description and remains not_formalized. B evidence used for these expressions remains unreviewed under the experiment policy; formalization_origin=source denotes faithful transcription of supplied quotations, not independent source verification.","bindings":[{"object_id":"x_e_ij","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area","clearing.social_welfare"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R001-E001"],"rationale":"Bilateral inter-microgrid energy exchange e_{i,j}^t is a P1 decision variable and the quantity constrained by the market-clearing equation (15) and the inter-microgrid power balance (17); it links the social-cost minimization objective to inter-area clearing."},{"object_id":"x_e_i","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area","clearing.social_welfare"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R001-E001"],"rationale":"The energy-trading vector e_i belongs to the P1 variable set and is the object whose residual from its auxiliary version defines the P1 terminal condition."},{"object_id":"x_e_hat_i","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area","coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R001-E002"],"rationale":"The auxiliary energy-trading vector ê_i is updated in the higher-level P1 problem by the virtual clearing house; the coordination-to-inter-area edge represents the clearing-house coordination of the distributed P1 clearing iteration."},{"object_id":"x_g_i","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area","clearing.social_welfare"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R001-E001"],"rationale":"Local wind power supply g_i^t is a P1 decision variable and appears on the supply side of the inter-microgrid power balance (17)."},{"object_id":"x_q_i","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area","clearing.social_welfare"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R001-E001"],"rationale":"The main-grid power schedule q_i is a P1 decision variable and appears in the operating-cost objective and the power balance."},{"object_id":"x_q_b_i","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area","clearing.social_welfare"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R001-E001"],"rationale":"Main-grid purchase quantity q_{b,i}^t is a P1 decision variable and appears on the supply side of the inter-microgrid power balance (17)."},{"object_id":"x_q_s_i","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area","clearing.social_welfare"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R001-E001"],"rationale":"Main-grid sale quantity q_{s,i}^t is a P1 decision variable and appears on the demand side of the inter-microgrid power balance (17)."},{"object_id":"x_r_c_i","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area","clearing.social_welfare"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R001-E001"],"rationale":"Storage charging power r_{c,i}^t is a P1 decision variable and appears on the demand side of the inter-microgrid power balance (17)."},{"object_id":"x_r_d_i","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area","clearing.social_welfare"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R001-E001"],"rationale":"Storage discharging power r_{d,i}^t is a P1 decision variable and appears on the supply side of the inter-microgrid power balance (17)."},{"object_id":"x_x_n","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area","clearing.social_welfare"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R001-E001"],"rationale":"Elastic load x_n^t is a P1 decision variable and appears in the aggregate elastic-load term of the inter-microgrid power balance (17)."},{"object_id":"theta_M","node_ids":["clearing.social_welfare","clearing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R001-E001"],"rationale":"M is the index set of microgrids summed in P1's social operating cost and the set over which inter-area clearing quantities are defined."},{"object_id":"theta_T","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"T is the set of time slots over which the inter-area clearing constraints (15) and (17) are imposed."},{"object_id":"theta_N_i","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"N_i is the set of users whose elastic loads are aggregated in the inter-microgrid power balance (17)."},{"object_id":"theta_eps1","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area","coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R001-E002"],"rationale":"The error tolerance epsilon_1 defines the terminal condition of the P1 clearing iteration coordinated by the virtual clearing house."},{"object_id":"theta_b_i","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"Aggregate inelastic load b_i^t is an exogenous demand input on the demand side of the inter-microgrid power balance (17)."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R001-C001","node_ids":["clearing.social_welfare","clearing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R001-E001"],"rationale":"C001 is the P1 social operating cost minimization linking the social-welfare node to the inter-area clearing node; frozen edge E001 carries C001."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R001-C002","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator","clearing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R001-E002"],"rationale":"C002 is the virtual clearing house coordination during P1; frozen edge E002 links the local operator coordination to inter-area clearing."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R001-C003","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C003 is the bilateral energy-clearing constraint (15) on inter-area allocations; no dedicated frozen edge exists for this constraint."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R001-C004","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C004 is the inter-microgrid power-balance constraint (17); it is an inter-area clearing constraint represented by the clearing.inter_area node."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R001-C005","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area","coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R001-E002"],"rationale":"C005 is the P1 iteration terminal condition for the auxiliary/actual energy-trading residual; it belongs to the clearing-house-coordinated decentralized P1 clearing procedure."}],"revision_requests":["Independent B source audit is still required for the B evidence behind equations (15), (17), and the P1 terminal condition; the candidate_support_evidence_ids are unreviewed in this experiment and no source correctness is claimed.","C001's P1 feasible set references constraints (1)-(3), (5), (6), (8)-(11), (14), (15), and (17); constraints (1)-(3), (5), (6), (8)-(11), and (14) are outside the supplied evidence partition for this rule, so their mathematical forms are not re-expressed here.","C002 remains not_formalized because the frozen statement scopes the virtual clearing house's role but does not itself specify the update equations (21) and (22); if a mathematical coordination constraint is required, C.1 should add a source-supported constraint for those updates."]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
